hare-lsp's overlay patch for stdlib hare::parse::import.ha.

Sibling of parse.ha.patch; applied by the Makefile's hare/parse/.stamp
target on top of a fresh copy of $(STDLIB)/hare/parse/import.ha.

What it changes:
  In imports(), set each `import.loc.end` to the location of the closing
  `;` token, captured via `lex::prevloc(lexer)` right after the `;` is
  consumed - replacing the stdlib's post-parse `mkloc(lexer)`.

Why:
  Stdlib sets `loc.end` via mkloc(lexer) after the `use` statement
  finishes parsing. mkloc returns the position of the lexer's NEXT real
  token, and the LSP parses without COMMENTS mode, so "next token" can be
  code several lines below the import, across blank lines and intervening
  `//` comments. That makes loc.end extend over those comments.
  Downstream, textDocument/documentLink emits one link per import using
  `imp.loc.end.off`, so the link's range covers the import AND every
  comment until the next decl - VS Code then renders the entire first
  comment after each `use` block as a cmd+clickable underline that
  navigates into the imported module. Folding-range, unused-import code
  actions, and a few related features have the same problem.

  `lex::prevloc(lexer)` returns the location of the last consumed rune,
  so capturing it immediately after the closing `;` yields that `;`'s
  position without the lookahead. We read it per-branch, before any
  intervening `mkloc()` (which peeks and would advance the lexer state)
  can run.

Why prevloc rather than the matched token's own location:
  An earlier version of this patch stored `want(...)?.2` (the matched
  token's location field) into a `lex::location { ... }` local. That
  read correctly on the local toolchain but produced layout-dependent
  garbage on Alpine's hare=0.26.0.1-r0 (musl) - loc.end sometimes pointed
  at the next token instead of the `;`, so the unused-import quickfix
  range check fired on the wrong line. Reading lexer state through
  `lex::prevloc` is exactly how the stdlib's own loc_from() computes
  every other decl/expr end location, so it stays correct across stdlib
  and toolchain variants.

To refresh for a new Hare release: same procedure as parse.ha.patch;
see hare/parse/README.md.

Apply with: `patch -N -p1 -d hare/parse < hare/parse/import.ha.patch`

--- a/import.ha
+++ b/import.ha
@@ -43,6 +43,15 @@
 		switch (want(lexer, ltok::SEMICOLON, ltok::LBRACE,
 			ltok::EQUAL, ltok::TIMES)?.0) {
 		case ltok::SEMICOLON =>
+			// hare-lsp patch (see import.ha.patch): capture loc.end
+			// at the ';' just consumed via lex::prevloc (location of
+			// the last consumed rune), before the mkloc() below peeks
+			// the next real token and drags loc.end across blank
+			// lines and comments. prevloc reads lexer state the same
+			// way the stdlib's loc_from() does for every other end
+			// location, so it stays correct across stdlib variants -
+			// unlike copying the matched token's own location field.
+			import.loc.end = lex::prevloc(lexer);
 			synassert(mkloc(lexer), !trailing,
 				"Unexpected trailing :: in ident")?;
 		case ltok::LBRACE =>
@@ -50,6 +59,7 @@
 				"Expected trailing :: in ident")?;
 			import.bindings = name_list(lexer)?;
 			want(lexer, ltok::SEMICOLON)?;
+			import.loc.end = lex::prevloc(lexer);
 		case ltok::EQUAL =>
 			synassert(mkloc(lexer),
 				len(name) == 1 && !trailing,
@@ -58,14 +68,16 @@
 			free(name);
 			import.ident = ident(lexer)?;
 			want(lexer, ltok::SEMICOLON)?;
+			import.loc.end = lex::prevloc(lexer);
 		case ltok::TIMES =>
 			synassert(mkloc(lexer), trailing,
 				"Expected trailing :: in ident")?;
 			import.bindings = ast::import_wildcard;
 			want(lexer, ltok::SEMICOLON)?;
+			import.loc.end = lex::prevloc(lexer);
 		case => abort(); // Unreachable
 		};
-		import.loc.end = mkloc(lexer);
+		// loc.end is set per-branch above, from each closing ';'.
 	};
 	return imports;
 };
